{
  "$schema": "https://aka.ms/codetour-schema",
  "title": "Langley Portfolio — New Joiner Onboarding",
  "description": "End-to-end walkthrough of the portfolio: Next.js 16 App Router, the editor-shell UI, and the Sanity CMS data + preview pipeline.",
  "ref": "main",
  "steps": [
    {
      "directory": "app",
      "title": "App Router Root",
      "description": "This is the Next.js 16 App Router root. Routes are split into three zones: `(site)` (public marketing pages behind the editor shell), `studio` (Sanity Studio at /studio, no shell), and `api` (draft + revalidate webhooks). The `(site)` parens make it a route group — URL stays clean but the layout composes differently. Gotcha: `/studio` deliberately sits outside `(site)` so it doesn't inherit the site chrome."
    },
    {
      "file": "app/layout.tsx",
      "line": 45,
      "title": "RootLayout — Providers Wire-Up",
      "description": "RootLayout is the outermost React boundary for every route. It loads Sanity site settings for metadata + favicon, then stacks providers in a specific order: ThemeProvider (next-themes) → CursorProvider (custom cursor variant state) → ToastProviderWrapper → TargetCursor (reactbits cursor FX) → SmoothScroll (Lenis) → PreviewBanner (only if draftMode on). Why it matters: any global UI concern belongs here, but route-specific chrome does NOT — that's the next file. Gotcha: `draftMode()` is awaited at the top level, which makes this layout dynamic; new joiners often try to make it static again and silently break preview."
    },
    {
      "file": "app/(site)/layout.tsx",
      "line": 19,
      "title": "Site Layout — The Editor Shell",
      "description": "This layout wraps only the marketing pages. It mounts `AppLayout` (the AE/VSCode-style 'editor shell' with MenuBar, SideDock, StatusBar, MobileDock), `ScrollProgress`, `CustomCursor`, and `CommandMenuWrapper` (Cmd+K). Why it matters: this is *the* visual identity — a portfolio that pretends to be a video editor / IDE. Gotcha: `siteSettings` is fetched here (server component) and passed down to Footer only; the shell itself reads it via context/hooks elsewhere. Don't try to pass it to AppLayout as props without checking how children consume it."
    },
    {
      "file": "components/shell/AppLayout.tsx",
      "line": 23,
      "title": "AppLayout — Shell Composition",
      "description": "This is the 'client' component that actually composes the editor chrome: MenuBar (top), SideDock (left vertical dock, desktop), StatusBar (bottom), MobileDock (bottom, mobile), and a `{children}` viewport in the middle. Why it matters: any change to the 'fake editor' illusion happens here or in these four subcomponents. Gotcha: responsive split is via Tailwind `hidden md:block` / `md:hidden` — there's no JS breakpoint logic, so don't add `useMediaQuery` if a class will do."
    },
    {
      "file": "app/(site)/page.tsx",
      "line": 16,
      "title": "Homepage — Data Flow Entry",
      "description": "The homepage is an async Server Component that fans out four Sanity fetches in `Promise.all` (siteSettings, featuredProjects, profile, tools) and feeds them into four section components: Hero, AboutSection, FeaturedProjectsSection, ToolsSection. `export const revalidate = 60` makes it ISR. Why it matters: this is the canonical data-loading pattern for the whole site — server fetch + prop drilling into presentational components, no client-side fetching. Gotcha: all four sections are server components; if you add interactivity, you need `'use client'` on the section, not here."
    },
    {
      "file": "sanity/lib/fetch.ts",
      "line": 184,
      "title": "Sanity Fetch Layer — Typed Wrappers",
      "description": "Every GROQ call goes through `sanityFetch<T>(query, params, preview)` which picks a client and returns a typed Promise. Public exports like `getSiteSettings`, `getFeaturedProjects`, `getProjectBySlug` are thin wrappers around it. The file also declares all shared TypeScript shapes (`SiteSettings`, `Project`, `ProjectCard`, etc.) — this is the source of truth for what Sanity returns. Why it matters: when you add a new content type, you add (1) the schema, (2) the GROQ query, (3) an interface here, (4) a typed wrapper here. Gotcha: the `preview` flag is threaded through every wrapper but rarely passed — most callers rely on draft-mode cookies at a higher level."
    },
    {
      "file": "sanity/lib/queries.ts",
      "line": 73,
      "title": "GROQ Queries — Featured Projects Example",
      "description": "All GROQ lives here, tagged with `/* groq */` for syntax highlighting. This featured-projects query shows the house style: filter on `_type`, project `slug.current` to a flat string, expand references with `->` (category, tools, coverImage.asset), and sort with `order()`. Why it matters: queries return *exactly* what the component tree needs — there's no ORM or DTO layer, so over-fetching here directly hurts page weight. Gotcha: `getAllProjectsQuery` uses the `!defined($type) || $type == \"\"` trick because GROQ params can't be null; the fetch wrapper converts `null` to `''` to match."
    },
    {
      "file": "sanity/lib/client.ts",
      "line": 51,
      "title": "Sanity Client Factory — Preview Duality",
      "description": "Two clients are created from one config: `client` (uses CDN, public published content) and `previewClient` (no CDN, `previewDrafts` perspective, needs `SANITY_API_READ_TOKEN`). `getClient(preview)` picks the right one. Why it matters: this is the whole preview-mode security model — drafts are only visible if you have the token, and token only ships server-side. Gotcha: `NEXT_PUBLIC_`-prefixed env vars ship to the browser, so the read token deliberately uses the unprefixed `SANITY_API_READ_TOKEN` — never add it as `NEXT_PUBLIC_`."
    },
    {
      "file": "sanity.config.ts",
      "line": 38,
      "title": "Sanity Studio Config — Singletons & Structure",
      "description": "Configures the embedded Studio at `basePath: /studio`. Wires `structureTool` with a custom desk structure, `visionTool` for GROQ playground, and a singleton plugin that gives `siteSettings`, `profile`, `faq` fixed document IDs and removes them from 'New document' / delete / duplicate actions. Why it matters: singletons are how the site gets exactly-one settings/profile — without these filters a content editor could accidentally create a duplicate `siteSettings` and the GROQ `[0]` would silently pick the wrong one. Gotcha: the schema `templates` override is easy to miss but is the only thing enforcing the fixed `_id`."
    },
    {
      "file": "app/api/draft/route.ts",
      "line": 23,
      "title": "Draft Mode API — Preview Entry Point",
      "description": "GET /api/draft?secret=...&slug=...&type=... is what Sanity Studio's 'Preview' button hits. It checks `SANITY_PREVIEW_SECRET`, calls `draftMode().enable()` (sets a cookie), and redirects to the right page based on document type. Why it matters: this is the only door into draft mode, and the redirect switch here decides whether a content editor lands on the right page after clicking Preview. Gotcha: there's a sibling `/api/draft/disable/` route — don't forget both when changing auth."
    },
    {
      "file": "app/api/revalidate/route.ts",
      "line": 28,
      "title": "Revalidate Webhook — Cache Busting",
      "description": "Sanity webhooks POST here on publish. The route validates `SANITY_WEBHOOK_SECRET`, reads `_type` and `slug` from the body, and calls `revalidatePath` for the affected URLs (e.g. project detail + `/projects` + homepage). Why it matters: this is how the site stays fresh despite ISR — without it, edits wait up to `revalidate` seconds to appear. The switch statement is the cache-invalidation matrix; if you add a new content type, you add a case here. Gotcha: `siteSettings` revalidates `'/'` with the `'layout'` arg — that's the layout-granular revalidate, not just the page, and it's load-bearing for the favicon/metadata update."
    },
    {
      "file": "app/(site)/projects/[slug]/page.tsx",
      "line": 42,
      "title": "Project Detail — Putting It All Together",
      "description": "The dynamic project page ties the whole pipeline together: `generateStaticParams` pre-renders all projects, `generateMetadata` builds OG images via `urlFor`, `revalidate = 300` for ISR, then the page itself fetches project + related projects server-side and hands them to `ProjectDetailEnhanced`. Why it matters: this is the template for any new dynamic route you'll add — copy this shape, not a hand-rolled alternative. Gotcha: `params` is `Promise<{ slug: string }>` in Next 15/16 — always `await params` before reading `slug`."
    },
    {
      "title": "Next Steps",
      "description": "You can now: (1) trace a request from URL → layout → server component → fetch wrapper → GROQ → Sanity, (2) add a new content type by touching schema → query → fetch wrapper → page → revalidate case, (3) debug preview issues knowing the token / draft-mode / CDN triangle. Open `components/home/Hero.tsx` next to see how presentational components consume the data shapes you just saw."
    }
  ]
}
